From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 09:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] h264_mvc: hand slots out and take them back in any order

vf_put() ignored which vframe came back and only moved putting_ptr on;
the recycle timer then returned the buffers of the slot at put_ptr, the
oldest one handed out. Receivers put frames back out of get order:
amlvideo puts a frame it drops (QBUF with V4L2_BUF_FLAG_DONE) while
amvideo still shows an older one, and amvideo puts the frames it skips
before the one in RDMA. The frame on screen, both views, was then
returned to the firmware and decoded into.

Retiring the ring in order instead is no fix: the frame on screen pins
every slot after it, a burst of drops fills the ring, the ISR then has
nowhere to publish, amvideo never toggles, never puts the frame it
holds, and playback freezes for good. So the ring goes, and slots are
used out of order, the way the other decoders' pools work:

- Each slot has one state: FREE, FILL (the ISR collects a picture's
  views in it), READY (published), GOT (handed out) or STALE.
- The ISR takes any FREE slot as the fill slot for a new picture. Views
  only pair there, so check_in_list()'s scan of the pool for a POC
  becomes a look at the fill slot, with a one-time warning should it be
  in any other state. Published slots go into a kfifo of slot indices;
  vf_peek() and vf_get() read its head, so frames still go out in
  decode order.
- vf_put() checks the vframe is from the pool and its slot GOT; a double
  put is ignored with a ratelimited warning. The slot drops its hold on
  both views' buffers and is FREE again at once.
- A buffer's use count, kept per view as before, decides when it is
  free: at zero it is queued in a per-view bitmap, and the recycle timer
  drains the bitmaps into BUFFER_RECYCLE, one buffer per write, only
  while the firmware has cleared the register, the views taking turns so
  a backlog in one does not hold the other back. A buffer the firmware
  hands out again before it went back is taken off the queue.
- A new picture releases whatever the fill slot still held of an older,
  unpaired one, and a view arriving again for the fill slot's picture
  releases the buffer it replaces. Both used to be overwritten, their
  use counts never dropped, and the buffers were lost to the firmware
  until the next restart.

The pool grows from 20 slots to one per buffer id (64). Every slot held
pins a buffer the firmware has not got back, and it has up to 24 per
view (16 for level 5.x plus the default margin of 8). With that margin a
free slot exists while the firmware still has a buffer to decode into;
a dynamic_buf_num_margin above 16 can run the pool out. If it does, the
view is dropped with a ratelimited warning, its buffer goes back through
the queue, and it is counted in view_dropped, once, as the ISR already
counts a view the firmware flagged. A view id other than 0 or 1, or a
buffer id beyond what was allocated for the view, is dropped the same
way instead of indexing the counts; a view 2 or 3 is only counted, as
there is no buffer of ours to return. do_alloc_work() caps view 0's
buffer count at buffer_spec_num, the size of buffer_spec0/1 fixed at
probe, before init_canvas() and the firmware see it, and gives view 1
the same count, as the box is laid out for two equal halves; the bmmu
box is sized from the same number, so a dynamic_buf_num_margin changed
after probe cannot index past buffer_spec0/1 or the box.

A restart re-initialises the pool, but the vframes are static: a frame a
receiver got before it still has the same address and slot. Its put
used to land on whatever the new session had in that slot. A stamp in
the vframe cannot tell the two apart, as the new session's get rewrites
the same vframe. Instead local_init marks the slots still handed out
STALE: their put frees them, and the ISR fills them when no slot is
FREE. amvideo keeps a copy of the frame it shows on unregister and never
puts it, so in practice a STALE slot is only reclaimed that way. The
same pass initialised display_pos to -1 and view0's buffer id to
DISPLAY_INVALID_POS, the wrong way round; a FREE slot no longer takes
part in pairing either way.

All state changes run under the lock the ISR and the timer already take:
put comes from the vsync interrupt and from process context. vf_peek()
takes it to read the kfifo head and a count of the clean pictures ready,
kept at publish and get, which send_drop_cmd() used to recount by
walking the ring; send_drop_cmd() becomes static, nothing else calls it.
set_frame_info() runs before the slot is published instead of after,
when a receiver could get the frame with the previous one's duration
and aspect. vf_states() reports the slots a picture can take (FREE or
STALE), the queued buffers and the kfifo length. The dbg_mode bit 4
path, which recycles every view without display, busy-waited on
BUFFER_RECYCLE and wrote it behind the timer's back; it goes through
the queue too, without counting the view as dropped.

A dirty frame (one the firmware flagged as dropped) is released like any
other once put; it was held until the next clean put. A picture
published without one of its views is marked dirty as well, matching
the count of clean pictures send_drop_cmd() reads. putting_ptr,
dirty_frame_num, enable_recycle, the timer's dead branch on the latter
and the #if 0 get_valid_frame() go, and the per-view fields become
arrays indexed by the view.

